No soul is beyond the reach of death. But some, it seems, have little interest in staying dead.

The giant steps forward, his silhouette swelling through the incense-filled haze, a morning star hanging loosely in one enormous hand. He moves with none of the frantic urgency of the men surrounding him. There is no roar, no battle cry to rally the spirits of the desperate. Only footsteps echo through the smoke—slow, heavy, and certain.

The nearest brigand turns just in time to see the weapon coming. The morning star catches him across the chest with brutal force, sending him sailing through the smoke until he crashes against the tavern's far wall hard enough to rattle the shelves behind the bar. Silence falls instantly. Even the patrons who had remained drinking finally look up from their tankards.

The giant lowers his weapon. For the first time, his face becomes visible through the thinning veil of smoke. He is old—not ancient, perhaps, but old enough that youth has long since abandoned him. His skin is weathered and scarred, marked by old wounds that have faded into pale lines across his jaw and brow. A thick beard hangs from his chin, uneven and streaked with grey, while several small metal rings pierce one ear.

His armor is little more than a collection of battered plates strapped together over thick leather. None of it matches, and none of it looks particularly well maintained. And yet every piece bears the same quiet history: the scars of battle, the wear of endless travel, and the stubborn proof of survival. This is not the armor of a soldier; it is the armor of someone who has simply refused to die.

The giant rolls his shoulder. "That one was annoying."

The smaller stranger, standing several paces away, glances toward the man now sprawled unconscious against the wall. "I thought you might say that."

The giant looks at him, then at the elf, and back again. "You are strange."

"I've been told."

The giant grunts as another brigand charges from the smoke. The elf turns, her longsword rising as steel meets steel. For an instant, the two stand locked together, but then the giant's massive hand closes around the brigand's collar, lifting him from the ground until his sword clatters harmlessly to the floor.

"Enough," the giant says, tossing the man aside to land beside his companion. Neither gets back up.

The rumble of violence is beginning to die, not because anyone has won, but because there is no one left willing to continue. The remaining patrons have vanished through the doors, leaving tables overturned and broken glass glittering beneath the smoke. What remains of the tavern's candles flicker weakly in the haze. The barmaid continues polishing a glass, watching the giant, then the elf, then the stranger.

"Anyone ordering anything?" she asks. No one answers. "Figures."

The elf slowly lowers her sword as the smoke begins to thin. The smaller stranger notices and takes a step forward, but the elf immediately raises her blade. He stops.

"Fair. You have been interfering," she says, her masked face tilting slightly. "Why?"

The question hangs between them. The stranger reaches beneath his coat, causing the giant's fingers to tighten around the haft of his weapon and the elf's sword to shift another inch upward. Slowly, deliberately, the stranger removes a folded piece of parchment and holds it between two fingers.

"I was sent to find you."

"By whom?" the elf demands.

"A person who prefers not to be found."

"Convenient."

"Very. And extremely annoying."

The elf stares at him, then at the parchment. "You expect me to believe you?"

"No," the stranger replies, a smile touching his lips. "I expect you to read it. They didn't give me a name. Just a description—plague mask, dark elven ears, and I'd find you in The Wandering Time Tavern. Said you'd be walking alone with steel still warm."

He tosses the parchment toward her. She catches it, noting the unfamiliar seal upon its surface—not royal, not religious, not belonging to any guild she recognizes. But it carries a symbol: a circle broken by three narrow lines. The elf's gloved thumb rests against it, and for the first time since the fighting began, she hesitates.

"The person who sent me needs you alive," the stranger says.

The giant snorts. "That's a flattering invitation."

"I don't believe anyone invited you," the stranger counters.

The giant's mouth twitches. "Good." He rests the morning star against his shoulder. "My name is Torvald."

"That was unexpectedly straightforward," the stranger blinks.

"I don't like making introductions complicated." Torvald's gaze returns to the elf. "I also don't like watching people get three on one. They were cowards."

"They were going to kill her," the stranger notes.

"I noticed. And yet you're helping her." Torvald shrugs. "I suppose I am."

"That wasn't part of the plan," the stranger admits.

"What plan?" Torvald asks.

"You were not included."

"Then your plan was flawed."

"Apparently." Torvald looks toward the tavern doors. Outside, the storm has weakened; rain still whispers against the windows, but the thunder has drifted farther away. The room no longer trembles beneath it. The tavern exhales.

What remains is the aftermath: broken chairs, cracked mugs, blood soaking into old wood, and three strangers standing together in the wreckage as though some invisible hand has placed them there deliberately.

The elf finally unfolds the parchment. Her eyes move across the writing, and though the stranger cannot see her expression behind the mask, he sees the way her fingers tighten.

"You know what this means?" he asks.

"Some of it."

"And?"

She folds the parchment again. "They are asking for my help."

"Yes. For what?" His smile fades. "Something is happening beyond these roads."

Torvald looks between them. "That sounds unpleasant."

"It is."

"Then I'll come."

The stranger looks at him sharply. "No. You won't."

Torvald considers this, then shrugs. "Alright."

The stranger blinks. "Alright?"

"I'll come anyway."

For the first time, something resembling genuine amusement reaches the stranger's eyes. "You're going to be a problem."

"I usually am."

The elf sheathes her sword, the sound of steel sliding into leather strangely final. She steps toward the table where her cloak lies crumpled among broken glass. "You should leave before dawn," she tells the stranger.

"Why?"

"Because I have a feeling this tavern won't be the same tomorrow."

The elf drapes the cloak over her shoulders. "Neither will we."

Torvald picks up his morning star. The barmaid sets three untouched mugs upon the nearest table. "You're all leaving," she says. It is not a question.

The stranger looks at the drinks, then at her. "How much?"

"Three silver."

Torvald reaches into one of his enormous pockets and produces a single gold coin. The barmaid looks at it, then at him. "Really?"

Torvald nods. She takes it. "Keep the change."

He does. The stranger shakes his head. "You've just paid ten times too much."

"Then perhaps she will remember us," Torvald replies as he opens the door.

Cold air spills into the tavern. The rain has nearly stopped. Beyond the doorway, the road disappears into darkness. The elf steps outside first, followed by the stranger, with Torvald ducking beneath the frame to come last.

The stranger pauses just outside the threshold, the cold wind tugging at his coat. He glances back at the elf, who stands a few paces ahead, her silhouette framed by the fading light of the tavern's lanterns.

"One thing," he calls out softly.

She turns, her masked face catching the dim glow.

"They gave me a description to find you. They didn't give me your name." He gestures vaguely. "I'd prefer to know what to call you when this inevitably goes sideways."

The elf doesn't answer right away. She studies him through the eyeholes of her plague mask, unreadable as ever.

|| "Ask me again tomorrow," she says.

And she walks into the dark without looking back. ||
